WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:09.129
I'm picturing this as a kind of 007 entry.

00:00:09.129 --> 00:00:13.919
Both the Republican and Democratic establishments
were shaken by the New Hampshire primary,

00:00:13.919 --> 00:00:18.590
which had Donald Trump and Bernie Sanders
clearly and convincingly in the lead.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:22.849
The Democratic establishment will stay in
Hilary Clinton's corner, especially since

00:00:22.849 --> 00:00:27.489
there still remains a doubt about whether
Sanders can win Black and Hispanic Democrats

00:00:27.489 --> 00:00:28.710
in large numbers.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:33.440
He didn't have to do that to win New Hampshire,
but he would have to do it to win the Democratic

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:34.440
nomination.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.219
The situation's a lot murkier on the Republican
side.

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:43.129
Republicans still don't want Donald Trump
as their nominee for the most part, but the

00:00:43.129 --> 00:00:47.460
party establishment isn't sure who to get
behind instead.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.449
Ted Cruz who beat Trump in Iowa, is disliked
by many members of the party establishment,

00:00:52.449 --> 00:00:58.260
and the party establishment hasn't decided
whether it prefers John Kasich, Marco Rubio,

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.809
or Jeb Bush to be their champion.

00:01:01.809 --> 00:01:06.490
South Carolina will tell us which of them
that they pick, and set up the possibility

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:09.490
of a three person race going forward.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:12.680
It's right on zero.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.140
Do you think that Sanders and Trump will win
the nomination, or will the establishment

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.140
ultimately prevail?

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.439
Let us know in the comments, and let us know
also if you think there are any other topics

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:23.690
that AEI scholars should cover in 60 seconds.

